          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does asthma guidance describe as the stepwise approach, and what changed about reliever choice?",
          "answer": "### Asthma Management: Stepwise Approach\n\nThe stepwise approach to managing asthma involves tailoring treatment based on the severity of symptoms, control, and exacerbation history. It is organized into steps to provide escalating treatment more effectively as needed. According to the guideline from the Global respiratory strategy report and national asthma panel, all adults and adolescents with asthma should receive inhaled corticosteroid therapy. Reliever-only treatment is no longer deemed appropriate at any severity of asthma. \n\nThe guidance emphasizes that relying solely on a short-acting bronchodilator does not address underlying inflammation, which can lead to severe exacerbations, even for those with infrequent symptoms. The step-wise therapy encourages a regular review of treatment, adjusting according to symptom control and exacerbation frequency (source: *Asthma: confirming the diagnosis and the stepwise approach to controller therapy*).\n\n### Changes in Reliever Choice\n\nRecent guidelines have altered the options for asthma relievers. There is currently debate between authorities: the Global strategy guidance recommends an inhaled corticosteroid-and-formoterol combination as the preferred reliever for all steps of asthma management. In contrast, some national guidelines continue to support the use of short-acting bronchodilators as appropriate relievers for mild asthma alongside regular controller therapy.\n\nThis divergence indicates that while there is a trend towards more effective daily management with combination therapies, some guidelines still retain the traditional approach for milder cases (source: *Asthma: confirming the diagnosis and the stepwise approach to controller therapy*).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
